cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or the cached data, please cite the archived release."
title: "Tactical cash reserve versus dollar-cost averaging: backtest code and cached data (Taiwan 0050, United States SPY, South Africa STX40)"
abstract: >-
  Reproducible backtest of a tactical cash reserve rule against investing every
  periodic contribution immediately. The rule diverts a fixed share of each
  contribution into an interest-bearing reserve and deploys the reserve in full
  the first time the market closes 15 percent or more below its trailing 52-week
  high.

  One market-agnostic engine, one set of fixed parameters and one code path are
  applied to Taiwan (Yuanta Taiwan Top 50 ETF, 0050, with the Bank of Taiwan
  savings deposit rate), the United States (SPDR S&P 500 ETF Trust, SPY, with the
  FDIC national rate on savings deposits) and South Africa (Satrix 40, STX40, with
  the IMF International Financial Statistics deposit rate) over 2 January 2009 to
  3 August 2026, chosen because their deposit rates differ by more than an order of
  magnitude. Setting the allocation ratio to zero reproduces the dollar-cost
  averaging control from the same function, so any reported gap reflects the
  allocation decision rather than a second implementation.

  The archive contains the documented companion notebook that reproduces every
  table and figure, the cached total-return price series and savings-deposit rate
  series together with the raw source files they were compiled from, the
  publication figure script, and a probability of backtest overfitting analysis by
  combinatorially symmetric cross-validation at 10, 16 and 20 blocks with the
  block-level diagnostics behind it.

  Version 1.2.0 adds the significance testing the analysis previously lacked: the
  Ledoit-Wolf studentized bootstrap for differences of Sharpe ratios with a HAC
  standard error from the delta method, Newey-West t-tests on mean weekly return
  differences, and within-market regressions of the weekly cost of the reserve on
  the concurrent deposit rate, pooled with market fixed effects.
